#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076ccae2-d102-5ad8-b1c2-ac28978e3e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263068} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3882de-2108-52bf-b5e1-4c3304b9a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a0770-0459-500c-aa32-352ae1667e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264813} {:answer/id #uuid "d493ac59-d9ae-5896-bd9e-006d111d56b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7e101e-fe81-5db4-a578-c892744685d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80428ab-daca-506e-aca7-d4e40b0d604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00dfea87-0564-54b8-b2ba-b85b9b81f898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263009} {:answer/id #uuid "02bdc94f-aecb-598c-8c8e-fd84d09e16d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=y-2=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263024} {:answer/id #uuid "2143561b-a016-53c4-935f-4521d8cb87c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{y-2}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263298} {:answer/id #uuid "71e1bc5e-929a-5149-b69e-6390e2a09556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263985} {:answer/id #uuid "cd91307b-95b0-5c36-965f-60e3ce8f9695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd91307b-95b0-5c36-965f-60e3ce8f9695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8f2342-92e1-5c88-9098-f4db5aaee8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46c7ac33-5b38-5319-85fa-4f19aa20d07b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131130" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $x={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(3,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496664} {:answer/id #uuid "132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-3y+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496700} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496894} {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x-4y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b9919e-c058-55c4-a73e-14ca053dbdce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb219d72-040d-55dd-90ca-3f831acb4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65173" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17754529-babb-5cf0-8d95-1b9cc9537939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336252} {:answer/id #uuid "2027b715-6614-5f66-82b2-1f71af6cb775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+\\frac{1}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336332} {:answer/id #uuid "36a67130-4f88-5f7d-9e66-e328e09a4789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336538} {:answer/id #uuid "530705a9-532d-591f-bbf7-492c24eb6728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336798} {:answer/id #uuid "8a797477-b113-50ff-8e91-509061d9a654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a797477-b113-50ff-8e91-509061d9a654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280} :answer-field/id #uuid "380e5863-1f99-5c29-acf5-cfab37a637d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7aa18077-2806-56e2-99dd-f7365f248356" :question/math-academy-id "q-71403" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201fe908-79f1-5c96-8882-603760dea14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513619} {:answer/id #uuid "3f49c5b4-7cf8-5c1b-9bb1-15cfaacad0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513700} {:answer/id #uuid "45b310bb-a17f-5c57-88ef-88eaeb7ca1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513719} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb3285b-1658-5c85-a2b4-99659e2beceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513831} {:answer/id #uuid "92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e47ae0-7d67-5cf0-9030-1e73b5fe057a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" :question/math-academy-id "q-71405" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,k \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz))\\text{ }j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(-(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0))\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$"}]}
